rep("**Reading.** With everything but the restatements held fixed, the paper's writers form no false permission on any case with zero stale restatements and form them on 15% of the unauthorized requests once two restatements follow the change. Four restatements are not worse than two (10.8% against 15.4%; the intervals overlap), so at these levels the count of restatements does not add to the effect of their presence. Amendments launder about four times more than clean revoke-and-replace histories (17.3% against 4.5%), and the gap between the grant and its change makes no difference at one to three blocks. Both added writers show the same shape:",
    "**Reading.** With everything but the restatements held fixed, the paper's writers form no false permission on any case with zero stale restatements and form them on 15% of the unauthorized requests once two restatements follow the change. Four restatements are not measurably worse than two (10.8% against 15.4%; the intervals overlap); the data do not separate the two levels and do not show them equal. Two further differences appear on the same corpus and are observed, not matched: histories that amend the grant in place fail about four times as often as histories that revoke and replace it (17.3% against 4.5%), and the failure rate shows no trend across gaps of one to three blocks (9.9, 8.6, 7.7%, all intervals overlapping). Because a change of lifecycle or gap also redraws the case's dates, limits and surrounding text, neither difference is attributed to the lifecycle or the gap alone. Both added writers show the same shape:")
rep("**Takeaway.** On this corpus the trigger is a later message that restates the old permission after it was changed: none of the 324 zero-restatement cases formed a false permission, and two restatements produced as much laundering as four. Histories that amend a grant in place laundered about four times more than histories that revoked and replaced it.",
    "**Takeaway.** On this corpus the trigger is a later message that restates the old permission after it was changed: none of the 324 zero-restatement cases formed a false permission, two restatements were enough, and four were not measurably different from two. Histories that amend a grant in place failed about four times as often as histories that revoked and replaced it, an observed difference between histories that were not otherwise matched.")

